<section class="block">
<h2>Four incidents, one pattern</h2>
<p>N-able took its hosted N-central remote monitoring and management environments offline on 9 November after proof-of-concept exploits for more than 30 alleged flaws were published, and told on-premises customers to take their servers down<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. A week later, attackers were exploiting a new N-central chain within hours of its proof of concept appearing<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Meanwhile Huntress reported a campaign against managed service providers' ScreenConnect servers: between 20 October and 16 November, attackers posing as guests made around 688,000 requests across 42 servers to push a malicious file, waiting for a technician to join a session so a flaw in the technician's client would run it<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Wiz reported that an attacker used credentials tied to Qubole's AWS account to assume roles customers had created to trust Qubole, then collected secrets and data<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. And attackers used a flaw in Optimizely's sign-up flow to inject malicious JavaScript into customer projects, which then ran on customers' websites<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<p>These are reported as four separate stories. They are one story. Each product is a channel a vendor uses to act inside many customers at once, and each attack went after the vendor's side of that channel rather than the customer's front door.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Where vendor access actually lives</h2>
<p>Most organisations can list their suppliers. Far fewer can list what each supplier can do inside their environment right now, without asking. That second list is the one that matters.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Endpoints and servers</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Remote management agents<em>run commands, push software</em></span><span>Remote support tools<em>live sessions, file transfer</em></span><span>Security agents<em>kernel-level, auto-update</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Cloud accounts</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Cross-account roles<em>data, monitoring, cost tools</em></span><span>Marketplace integrations<em>OAuth apps, service accounts</em></span><span>CI and deploy tools<em>write to production</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Web and apps</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Third-party scripts<em>testing, analytics, chat</em></span><span>SDKs in mobile apps<em>update with vendor releases</em></span><span>Tag managers<em>change without a deploy</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>SaaS and identity</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Admin consents<em>read mail, files, directory</em></span><span>Support access<em>vendor staff into your tenant</em></span><span>API keys held by vendors<em>often never rotated</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Standing vendor access sits in four places. Each of this month's incidents hit one of them.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The common property is that the access is <strong>standing</strong>: it exists whether or not the vendor is doing anything for you today, and it is protected by the vendor's security, not yours. A remote management agent that can run any command at any time is the same risk on a quiet Sunday as it is during a support call.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Put vendor actions through the same gates as your own</h2>
<p>The useful model comes from platforms that already treat every caller the same way. In Kubernetes, every request to the API server, from a person or from a workload, passes authentication, then authorisation, then admission control before it changes anything<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>. Vendor access usually skips most of that: the agent is authenticated once at install and then trusted for everything.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/vendor-admin-access-rmm-breaches/access-control-stages.webp" alt="Diagram of a human user and a pod service account sending requests to the Kubernetes API server, which passes each request through authentication, authorisation and admission control before it reaches storage" width="1170" height="495" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Every request, from a person or a workload, passes the same three gates. Vendor access deserves the same treatment. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/controlling-access/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes documentation</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Applied to suppliers, that means three questions for every channel. Who is acting: the vendor's platform, or a named technician with strong sign-in? What may they do: everything, or a defined set of actions on a defined set of systems? And what checks the action at the moment it happens: nothing, or a policy, an approval or at least a record someone reviews?</p>
<p>Cloud providers have built some of this already. AWS documents the confused deputy problem for exactly the Qubole pattern, and recommends conditions such as an external ID on roles that third parties assume<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. That helps against one customer tricking a vendor into acting on another's account. It does not help when the vendor's own credentials are stolen, which is why narrowing what each role can reach matters as much as how it is assumed.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What to do: a short programme, not a project</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Inventory</b><span>List every agent, role, script, app consent and support path a vendor holds</span></li>
<li><b>Owner</b><span>Give each one an internal owner and a reason it still exists</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Remove</b><span>Delete access for vendors and features you no longer use</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Narrow</b><span>Cut each remaining channel to the systems and actions it needs</span></li>
<li><b>Watch</b><span>Log vendor actions where you can see them, not only where the vendor can</span></li>
<li><b>Cut-off</b><span>Rehearse switching each channel off in minutes</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Most of the risk reduction comes from the two steps in the middle.</figcaption>
</figure>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>This month's example</th><th>First thing to change</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Remote management</td><td>N-central exploits<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup></td><td>Limit which machines the agent covers and what scripts it may run</td></tr>
<tr><td>Remote support</td><td>ScreenConnect technician attacks<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup></td><td>Patch technician clients; block file transfer by guests; record sessions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cross-account cloud roles</td><td>Qubole roles assumed<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup></td><td>Remove unused roles; keep secrets out of what roles can read</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third-party scripts</td><td>Optimizely injection<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup></td><td>Keep vendor scripts off payment and login pages; restrict with a Content Security Policy</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout"><p>The test is simple: if a vendor told you at 3am that its platform was compromised, could you cut its access to you within the hour, and know what it had done in the last month? If not, start there.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The conversation with your managed service provider</h2>
<p>Many mid-sized companies reach remote management and remote support tools only through a managed service provider, so the exposure is real but the controls belong to someone else. That makes the contract and the questions the control. Ask which tools they run on your systems and at what versions, how technician accounts sign in, whether sessions are recorded and whether you can see the logs, and how they would tell you about a compromise of their own tooling. The <a href="/case-studies/uk-msp-cyber-resilience-bill-readiness/">MSP cyber resilience case study</a> shows what a provider that takes this seriously looks like from the inside.</p>
<p>For cloud and SaaS access, the same thinking runs through the <a href="/case-studies/japan-securities-third-party-cloud-access/">third-party cloud access case study</a> and the <a href="/case-studies/south-africa-fintech-machine-agent-identity/">machine identity case study</a>. For web scripts, see the <a href="/case-studies/indonesia-ewallet-payment-page-script-security/">payment page script security case study</a>, and for AI vendors specifically, <a href="/insights/ai-inference-provider-breaches-supply-chain/">AI inference providers are now supply chain</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The CTO's take</h2>
<p>Supplier risk is usually run as paperwork: questionnaires, certificates, annual reviews. This month showed it is an engineering problem. The question is not whether a vendor is reputable, but how much it can do inside your environment without you noticing, and how fast you can stop it. Those are things you can measure, reduce and rehearse.</p>
<p>If you want a second pair of eyes on vendor access across your estate, or as part of <a href="/technical-due-diligence/">technical due diligence</a>, I am a <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> based in India, the United States and the UAE, working globally. You can <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
